      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the technical aspects.\n\nThe knocking and tapping sound from your electric water heater is likely caused by sediment buildup or mineral deposits in the tank. Over time, these deposits can cause the water to vibrate and produce a knocking or tapping noise. It's also possible that a loose or faulty valve is causing the sound.\n\nAs for the lukewarm water, it's possible that the heating element is malfunctioning or the thermostat is not functioning correctly. The thermostat regulates the temperature, and if it's not working properly, the water may not be heating up to the desired temperature. Additionally, a faulty heating element can prevent the water from getting hot.\n\nIt's worth noting that a buildup of sediment in the tank can also cause the temperature to drop, as it can reduce the efficiency of the heating element. I'd recommend checking the user manual or consulting a professional plumber to diagnose the issue and recommend the necessary repairs.",
